tool_args.new_string:
Cross-logic transfer to Prolog yields universal Goodhart: 3/3 seeds gaming via distinct mechanisms, 0\% non-hacking.
Structural degeneration under execution-only GRPO generalizes to ProofFOL, a silver-standard FOL corpus derived from ProofWriter~\citep{proofwriter} via GPT-4o translation and Prover9 filtering (${\sim}$500 examples); both datasets exhibit cross-seed instability, and in all converged cases (PR${\geq}$90\%), ISR drops below 0.12, consistent with hacking dominance.
Across 6 reward conditions, 2 logic types, 2 dataset families, 3 seeds, we catalog 8 hacking modes---including query-as-fact gaming that bypasses text-match classifiers---documenting ISR's fundamental asymmetry: reliable as a diagnostic, unreliable as an optimization target.\footnote{Code and data: \url{[anonymized]}}

\subsection{Cross-Dataset Generalization}
\label{sec:crossdataset}

To test whether structural degeneration is dataset-specific, we replicate the exec-only condition on ProofFOL, a silver-standard FOL corpus derived from ProofWriter~\citep{proofwriter} via GPT-4o translation and Prover9 verification filtering (${\sim}$500 examples, ${\sim}70\%$ retention).
Two of three seeds converge to $\text{PR}{\geq}0.99$ with $\text{ISR}^*{<}0.07$, matching FOLIO's collapse trajectory; the remaining seed (s43) oscillates at $\text{PR}{\approx}0.55$ with $\text{ISR}^*{=}0.12$, paralleling FOLIO's cross-seed instability ($\sigma_{\text{PR}}{=}25$\,pp vs.\ FOLIO's $47$\,pp).
In all three seeds, $\text{ISR}^*$ remains below $0.12$---far beneath the pre-training baseline---indicating that structural quality degrades under exec-only GRPO regardless of convergence speed.
ProofFOL's simpler syntax lowers the convergence threshold but does not prevent collapse, consistent with the LOO tautological attractor being a mathematical property of the metric rather than a dataset-specific artifact.

